.TH DEDUPE 1 "July 2026" "sumtag 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
dedupe \- delete files from a cull tree that duplicate an actual tree
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B dedupe
.BI \-\-database " DB"
.RB [ \-\-delete ]
.RB [ \-\-allow\-mixed ]
.I ACTUAL CULL
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B dedupe
dismantles a redundant copy of a directory tree.
.I ACTUAL
is the copy being kept \(em it is never modified, ever.
.I CULL
is the redundant copy: every file in it whose digest duplicates a file in
the
.I corresponding
directory of
.I ACTUAL
is deleted, regardless of name, and directories emptied by this are removed
on the way back up. If cull holds three files matching one actual-side
witness, all three are deleted. What survives in cull is therefore always a
signpost \(em something unique, unknown, or suspicious \(em and the cull
root itself always survives, even empty, as a placeholder and a receipt.
.PP
.B dedupe
deletes files. It is an experimental companion to
.BR sumtag (1)
and works from a sumtag SQLite database that already knows both trees; it
never computes a checksum. A bare run is a full preview
.RB ( "would delete" ", " "would sweep" ", " "would rmdir" ,
with the database opened read-only); actual deletion requires the explicit
.B \-\-delete
option.
.SH THE SYNCHRONIZED WALK
Both trees are walked depth-first at the same time, locked in step by
relative path: while visiting
.IB ACTUAL /foo/bar
the walk is in
.IB CULL /foo/bar\c
\&, and it never descends into a subdirectory name that does not exist as a
real directory on both sides. Comparison and deletion are therefore flat,
one directory pair at a time: a cull file dies exactly when a same-digest
file sits in the corresponding actual directory \(em same name not
required, same relative directory required. Reorganised duplicates are out
of scope by design, in exchange for a comparison that can be verified by
looking at one directory pair at a time.
.PP
The one exception is the empty-directory carve-out: a cull-side
subdirectory with no real files anywhere beneath it (only ignorable junk
files such as
.BR .DS_Store ,
qualifying symbolic links, and empty directories) is swept even without an
actual-side counterpart \(em deleting it destroys nothing.
.SH WHAT IS DELETED, AND WHAT NEVER IS
Only plain files positively identified as duplicates are deleted, plus the
exit junk below, and only ever on the cull side.
.IP \(bu 3
Files the database does not know (never stamped or mirrored) are invisible:
never matched, never deleted, left in place even if that leaves their
directory standing.
.IP \(bu 3
Ignorable junk files
.RB ( .DS_Store )
never block a directory's removal, and are swept exactly when they are the
last thing standing before the directory is removed \(em never earlier.
.IP \(bu 3
Symbolic links are never followed and never digest-matched. A link is swept
at directory exit only if it is relative and points inside the cull tree
\(em where it points is what matters, not whether the target still exists,
since the deletion phase has usually just emptied it. An absolute link is
never deleted, wherever it points, and neither is any link escaping the
cull tree: such a link blocks its directory like any other survivor.
.IP \(bu 3
A cull directory containing an
.B @sumtag\-ignore
marker (see
.BR sumtag (1))
is fenced off: not descended, nothing inside it deleted, swept, or removed;
it blocks its parent like any survivor. A marker on the cull root itself is
honoured with a warning, and the run does nothing.
.SH TRUST MODEL
Stored digests are trusted; neither side is re-hashed. That is defensible
because deleting a true duplicate is reversible \(em the actual tree keeps
the bytes \(em but it only holds if the stored digests describe the current
bytes, so three vetoes gate every candidate, none of which read file
contents:
.IP \(bu 3
the database digest must equal the digest in the file's own extended
attribute, on both sides;
.IP \(bu 3
the file's live mtime must equal the mtime recorded in its extended
attribute, on both sides;
.IP \(bu 3
the cull file's live size must equal its witness's. XXH3-64 is a 64-bit
non-cryptographic hash, and the size check is free collision insurance: a
digest match with differing sizes is reported loudly as a mismatch, the
file is kept, and the run exits 2.
.PP
A cull-side file failing a veto is kept, with a warning; an actual-side
file failing a veto simply witnesses nothing.
.SH SAFETY CHECKS
All of the following are verified before anything is at risk:
.IP \(bu 3
Both roots must exist and be disjoint under
.BR realpath (3)
\(em not equal, and neither nested inside the other, so symbolic links
cannot disguise an overlap.
.IP \(bu 3
The database must already exist and must know both roots: each root's live
mount point must be recorded and file rows must be present beneath it. An
unmounted drive is an error, never a silent no-match.
.IP \(bu 3
If the two roots' rows carry more than one digest algorithm, the run is
refused unless
.B \-\-allow\-mixed
is given, because cross-algorithm duplicates would silently survive.
.IP \(bu 3
At the moment of deletion, if the cull file and its witness resolve to the
same path under
.BR realpath (3),
they are one directory entry reached through links; the deletion is refused
loudly (exit status 2), since deleting \(lqthe copy\(rq would delete the
only name. The same inode behind different real paths is a genuine hard
link \(em safe to delete, and noted.
.PP
Pass the roots spelled as sumtag scanned them: row lookups use the absolute
path as given, exactly as sumtag records paths, so a differently-spelled
root (for example through a symlinked path component) fails the
must-know-both-roots check with a clear error rather than matching nothing.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-database " DB"
Path to the sumtag SQLite database that knows both trees. Required.
.TP
.B \-\-delete
Actually delete. Without it, the run is a complete preview and the database
is opened read-only. Deleted files' database rows are removed in the same
run, committed per directory, so an interrupted run stays consistent.
.TP
.B \-\-allow\-mixed
Proceed even when the roots carry more than one digest algorithm.
Cross-algorithm duplicates silently survive; see
.B SAFETY CHECKS
above.
.SH RUN SUMMARY
Every run ends with a summary block: files deleted (or would-delete) and
their cumulative size, items swept, directories removed, files kept (broken
down as unique, unknown, or stale), directories fenced by
.B @sumtag\-ignore
markers, errors, and the database and roots in use. Interrupting a run with
Ctrl-C prints the same summary, prefixed by an
.B interrupted
line; the counters cover only completed work, and commits are per
directory, so the database matches what actually happened.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
nothing redundant was found.
.TP
.B 1
duplicates were found (deleted, or would-delete under preview).
.TP
.B 2
errors or a safety refusal prevented a complete, clean run.
.TP
.B 130
the run was interrupted by Ctrl-C (128 + SIGINT, the shell convention).
.SH EXAMPLES
Preview what dismantling a redundant backup copy would delete:
.PP
.EX
dedupe \-\-database /var/lib/sumtag.sqlite /data/proj /backup/proj\-old
.EE
.PP
Actually delete:
.PP
.EX
dedupe \-\-database /var/lib/sumtag.sqlite \-\-delete /data/proj /backup/proj\-old
.EE
.SH NOTES
.B dedupe
is experimental \(em and it is the dangerous companion: always preview
first. Both trees should be freshly stamped
.RB ( "sumtag \-\-sum \-\-database"
\&...) before a run, so the trust-model vetoes have current metadata to
check against; stale stamps cause files to be kept, never wrongly deleted.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sumtag (1),
.BR grouper (1)
.SH AUTHOR
The sumtag authors.
